# Target C — C96 common-chain caterpillar frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_common_chain_caterpillar_2026-09-01.md](../../reports/target_c_common_chain_caterpillar_2026-09-01.md).

C87's D staircase intervals all contain one common C80 chain train `tau_r`, and every
simple interval is represented by at least two active diameter pairs, one in each
hull-arc orientation. The chain diameter `za` is also separated by `tau_r`.

Therefore the complete source diameter graph crossing the cut `tau_r` contains at
least

`2D+1`

edges.

C90's train-cut theorem applies: the crossing diameter graph is bipartite, hence
acyclic because planar diameter cycles are odd, and every component is a caterpillar.
If it has `m_r` edges and `c_r` nontrivial components, its incident endpoint count is

`v_r=m_r+c_r`.

Thus

`q>=v_r>=2D+2`.

More exactly, writing `e_r=m_r-(2D+1)`,

`q-(2D+2) >= e_r+(c_r-1)`.

So endpoint surplus pays for every extra common-cut diameter edge and every extra
caterpillar component. At the minimum `q=2D+2`, the common-cut graph is one spanning
caterpillar tree with exactly the chain edge plus the two selected orientations over
all D staircase edges.

In C94's low-train phase `T/D<3+o(1)`, C82 also gives

`q/D <= 2.1862006358...+o(1)`,

so this common-cut caterpillar already consumes almost the entire diameter-endpoint
budget.

The next target is a geometric classification of this near-spanning equal-length
caterpillar, especially the minimum-slack regime `q=2D+2+o(D)`.